Here's the formal test smell audit of your integration test suite using the **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog**, with integration-test calibration applied.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1 · **.NET 10** · Class marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000); // ← blocks for 3 seconds with no retry/polling
```

**Why it's still High even for integration tests:** The calibration rules downgrade Mystery Guest and Eager Test for integration suites, but *not* Sleepy Test — arbitrary fixed delays cause flakiness regardless of scope.

**Fix:**
```csharp
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    await Task.Delay(100, cts.Token);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Non-deterministic failures on slow CI; unnecessarily slow suite.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** One branch can silently pass even when the other was intended. You can never know which assertion ran from the test result alone.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsBasicUser()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden testing gap — the `if` branch may never execute, giving false coverage.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← No assertion. Passes even if all inserts silently fail.
}
```

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — test counts toward coverage but verifies nothing.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

4 assertions with no explanatory messages. When one fails in CI, the stack trace points to the method but not which property was wrong.

**Fix — add messages:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated ID should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure triage — developers must read source to understand which property broke.

---

## ✅ Smell-Free Patterns (Well-Designed Tests)

The following 4 tests are **clean** and follow good integration-test design:

| Method | Why it's good |
|--------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange/act/assert; focused; appropriate scope |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single behavior, negative assertion (null check) |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count contextually obvious (3 inserts → 3 count); uses `CollectionAssert` |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Tests a real concurrent scenario; asserts the outcome |

The `[TestInitialize]` fixture is also well-scoped — both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by **every** test method, so there is **no General Fixture smell**.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` | 2 min |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Split into 2 typed tests | 10 min |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling loop | 10 min |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | Add message strings to 4 assertions | 3 min |

Start with **#1** — it's the highest risk-to-effort ratio (zero verification today, one-line fix).
